1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,528
Throw a ball at twenty metres per second.

2
00:00:02,528 --> 00:00:08,882
At fifteen degrees it skims low; at seventy five it climbs high and drops close by.

3
00:00:08,882 --> 00:00:13,678
In between, forty five degrees sends it furthest of all, forty metres.

4
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:21,935
And notice: fifteen and seventy five degrees land at exactly the same spot, and so do thirty and sixty.

5
00:00:21,935 --> 00:00:23,667
Mechanics explains why.

6
00:00:23,667 --> 00:00:28,564
This unit is about forces: when they balance, and how they move things.

7
00:00:34,033 --> 00:00:40,571
Welcome to Class 12 Mathematics, Unit 16: Mechanics, the alternative to Unit 15.

8
00:00:42,967 --> 00:00:44,271
Five parts.

9
00:00:44,271 --> 00:00:48,672
The resultant of forces, equilibrium and Lami's theorem.

10
00:00:48,672 --> 00:00:51,015
Moments and the inclined plane.

11
00:00:51,015 --> 00:00:52,851
Newton's laws of motion.

12
00:00:52,851 --> 00:00:55,950
Connected bodies, lifts and momentum.

13
00:00:55,950 --> 00:00:57,045
And projectiles.

14
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:09,826
Two forces pulling on one point have a single equivalent force, the resultant: the diagonal of the parallelogram they make.

15
00:01:09,826 --> 00:01:16,818
Five newtons and three newtons at sixty degrees combine into exactly seven.

16
00:01:16,818 --> 00:01:23,495
Close the angle and the resultant grows to eight; open it to one eighty and it shrinks to two.

17
00:01:24,700 --> 00:01:34,545
When three forces hold a point still, Lami's theorem links them: each force divided by the sine of the angle between the other two gives the same number.

18
00:01:34,545 --> 00:01:40,440
A hundred newton weight hangs from two strings at thirty and sixty degrees to the ceiling.

19
00:01:40,667 --> 00:01:49,744
The strings are at right angles to each other, so the weight sits opposite ninety degrees; the tensions sit opposite one fifty and one twenty.

20
00:01:49,744 --> 00:01:55,812
Lami gives fifty newtons in the shallow string and eighty six point six in the steep one.

21
00:01:55,812 --> 00:01:57,692
The steeper string carries more.

22
00:01:58,900 --> 00:02:01,289
Equilibrium of three forces.

23
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:06,800
The third force balances the other two, so the resultant of three and four is five.

24
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,364
So cos theta is zero: the forces are at right angles.

25
00:02:11,364 --> 00:02:13,346
A three four five triangle.

26
00:02:18,933 --> 00:02:21,018
A force also turns things.

27
00:02:21,018 --> 00:02:26,018
Its moment about a point is the force times its perpendicular distance from the point.

28
00:02:26,018 --> 00:02:33,634
A four metre beam weighing two hundred newtons rests on two supports, with a three hundred newton load one metre from A.

29
00:02:33,867 --> 00:02:38,856
Take moments about A, so the unknown R A has no turning effect.

30
00:02:38,856 --> 00:02:43,634
Four R B equals two hundred times two plus three hundred times one.

31
00:02:43,634 --> 00:02:46,655
R B is a hundred and seventy five newtons.

32
00:02:46,655 --> 00:02:51,603
The vertical forces balance, so R A is three twenty five.

33
00:02:51,603 --> 00:02:54,295
The support nearer the load carries more.

34
00:02:55,500 --> 00:03:00,007
On a slope, resolve along the plane and perpendicular to it.

35
00:03:00,007 --> 00:03:11,179
A twenty kilogram block on a smooth thirty degree slope: its weight, two hundred newtons, splits into mg sin alpha down the slope and mg cos alpha into it.

36
00:03:11,179 --> 00:03:16,726
So holding it needs a hundred newtons up the slope, and the plane pushes back with a hundred root three.

37
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:23,207
Newton's three laws.

38
00:03:23,207 --> 00:03:26,072
Without a net force, nothing changes.

39
00:03:26,072 --> 00:03:30,629
With one, the acceleration is the force divided by the mass.

40
00:03:30,629 --> 00:03:35,772
And forces come in equal and opposite pairs, acting on different bodies.

41
00:03:35,772 --> 00:03:41,644
For constant acceleration, three equations connect velocity, distance and time.

42
00:03:42,867 --> 00:03:47,673
A ten kilogram block is pulled across a rough floor with forty newtons.

43
00:03:47,673 --> 00:03:56,475
Friction opposes the motion: the coefficient of friction times the normal reaction, a quarter of a hundred, twenty five newtons.

44
00:03:56,475 --> 00:04:02,889
The net force is fifteen newtons, so the acceleration is one and a half metres per second squared.

45
00:04:04,100 --> 00:04:05,478
Newton's second law at work.

46
00:04:05,700 --> 00:04:10,314
Acceleration four; after five seconds, twenty metres per second.

47
00:04:10,533 --> 00:04:14,657
It has gone fifty metres, and the work done is six hundred joules.

48
00:04:14,867 --> 00:04:17,395
Which equals the kinetic energy gained.

49
00:04:17,395 --> 00:04:19,438
Work turns into kinetic energy.

50
00:04:21,467 --> 00:04:22,245
Vertical motion.

51
00:04:22,700 --> 00:04:26,165
At the top the velocity is zero: twenty metres up.

52
00:04:26,367 --> 00:04:31,740
It takes two seconds to rise, and by symmetry two to fall: four in all.

53
00:04:37,333 --> 00:04:41,970
Five kilograms and three kilograms hang over a smooth pulley.

54
00:04:41,970 --> 00:04:48,884
The heavier side falls, the lighter rises, with the same acceleration and the same tension in the string.

55
00:04:48,884 --> 00:04:55,482
Write F equals m a for each mass, positive in its own direction of motion.

56
00:04:55,700 --> 00:05:02,681
Add the equations and the tension cancels: twenty equals eight a, so a is two and a half.

57
00:05:02,681 --> 00:05:08,201
Then the tension is thirty seven and a half newtons: between the two weights, as it must be.

58
00:05:09,433 --> 00:05:09,944
The lift.

59
00:05:10,667 --> 00:05:15,446
Going up faster, the floor must push harder: seven hundred and twenty newtons.

60
00:05:15,667 --> 00:05:19,231
Accelerating down, less: four hundred and eighty.

61
00:05:19,433 --> 00:05:23,958
In free fall the floor pushes not at all: you feel weightless.

62
00:05:25,967 --> 00:05:26,472
Momentum.

63
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:31,746
Momentum is conserved: twelve before, eight v after.

64
00:05:31,746 --> 00:05:33,697
One and a half metres per second.

65
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:38,185
Kinetic energy: twenty four joules before, nine after.

66
00:05:38,400 --> 00:05:40,926
Fifteen joules became heat and sound.

67
00:05:42,933 --> 00:05:43,466
Impulse.

68
00:05:44,167 --> 00:05:49,038
Take towards the wall as positive, so the rebound is minus fifteen.

69
00:05:49,038 --> 00:05:52,887
Impulse: minus five point two five newton seconds.

70
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:57,993
Spread over a twentieth of a second, the average force is a hundred and five newtons.

71
00:06:03,567 --> 00:06:06,550
A projectile does two things at once.

72
00:06:06,550 --> 00:06:14,871
Across, nothing pushes it, so its horizontal velocity stays fixed at u cos theta: the blue arrow never changes.

73
00:06:14,871 --> 00:06:23,869
Up and down, gravity slows it, stops it at the top, and brings it back: the pink arrow shrinks, vanishes and grows downward.

74
00:06:24,100 --> 00:06:40,234
From these come the formulas: time of flight two u sin theta over g, greatest height u squared sin squared theta over two g, and range u squared sin two theta over g. Sin two theta is largest at forty five degrees.

75
00:06:40,234 --> 00:06:49,268
And theta and ninety minus theta have the same sin two theta: that is why fifteen and seventy five degrees landed together.

76
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:51,584
The standard projectile.

77
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:56,174
Split the velocity: seventeen point three two across, ten up.

78
00:06:56,400 --> 00:07:02,694
Time of flight two seconds, height five metres, range thirty four point six metres.

79
00:07:02,900 --> 00:07:10,201
After one second the vertical velocity is zero: it is at the top, moving at seventeen point three two.

80
00:07:12,233 --> 00:07:13,244
Horizontal launch.

81
00:07:13,467 --> 00:07:20,494
Falling forty five metres from rest takes three seconds; meanwhile it travels sixty metres across.

82
00:07:20,700 --> 00:07:25,949
It lands with thirty downward and twenty across: thirty six point one metres per second.

83
00:07:27,967 --> 00:07:29,259
The cold open, explained.

84
00:07:29,467 --> 00:07:31,316
Sin two theta must be a half.

85
00:07:31,533 --> 00:07:38,985
So two theta is thirty or one fifty: fifteen or seventy five degrees, complementary angles.

86
00:07:39,200 --> 00:07:42,986
And the maximum, at forty five, is forty metres.

87
00:07:46,200 --> 00:07:46,970
Pause and think.

88
00:07:54,267 --> 00:08:00,990
Two point five: the difference in weight, twenty newtons, divided by the total mass, eight kilograms.

89
00:08:02,967 --> 00:08:03,402
One more.

90
00:08:11,033 --> 00:08:14,805
Forty five degrees, where sin two theta reaches one.

91
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:18,121
Quick recap.

92
00:08:18,121 --> 00:08:22,496
The parallelogram law and Lami's theorem for forces at a point.

93
00:08:22,496 --> 00:08:23,994
Moments for beams.

94
00:08:23,994 --> 00:08:27,054
Resolving on an inclined plane, and friction.

95
00:08:27,054 --> 00:08:30,999
Newton's laws, the equations of motion and momentum.

96
00:08:30,999 --> 00:08:35,985
And projectiles, with the greatest range at forty five degrees.

97
00:08:40,167 --> 00:08:41,601
That was mechanics.

98
00:08:41,601 --> 00:08:47,070
Next, the appendix: the retained topics you still need for the examination.

99
00:08:47,070 --> 00:08:47,660
See you there.

